= Medicine
== Day 01

=== Board 1 - CVS

==== ECG: Inferior MI (পেন্সিল দিয়ে মার্ক করতে হবে)
- ST elevation in leads: II, III, aVF
- Reciprocal ST depression in leads: I, aVL
- Culprit artery: RCA (80%) or LCx

==== ST Depression & ST Elevation (সংজ্ঞা ও পরিবর্তন)
- ST Elevation: >1mm in 2 contiguous limb leads, >2mm in chest leads
  - Causes: STEMI, Pericarditis, Early repolarization, Hyperkalemia
- ST Depression: >0.5mm below baseline
  - Causes: NSTEMI, Reciprocal change, LVH strain, Digoxin, Hypokalemia
- Evolution: Hyperacute T → ST elevation → Pathologic Q wave → T inversion

==== Drug Avoided in BOTH Anterior & Inferior MI
- Beta-blockers (Metoprolol, Atenolol) – AVOID if:
  - Heart failure, cardiogenic shock (SBP < 90)
  - PR >0.24s, 2nd/3rd degree AV block
  - Active bronchospasm
- Alternative: Start low-dose after 24 hours if stable

==== Blood Supply & SA Node, AV Node Location
- SA Node: Junction of SVC and right atrium (crista terminalis)
  - Supplied by: RCA (60%) or LCx (40%)
- AV Node: Inferior interatrial septum (Koch's triangle)
  - Supplied by: RCA (90%) or LCx (10%)
- LAD: Anterior LV, septum (V1-V4)
- LCx: Lateral LV (I, aVL, V5-V6)
- RCA: RV, inferior wall (II, III, aVF), SA/AV node

==== ECG: Anterior MI (পেন্সিল দিয়ে মার্ক করতে হবে)
- ST elevation in: V1-V4 (anteroseptal) or V1-V6 (extensive)
- Reciprocal ST depression in: II, III, aVF
- Culprit artery: LAD ("widow maker")
- Complications:
  - Heart failure (extensive LV damage) – most common cause of death in first 24h
  - Arrhythmias (VF/VT, LBBB, RBBB)
  - Cardiogenic shock (>40% LV involved)
  - Pericarditis (Dressler's – weeks later)
  - Ventricular aneurysm
  - Sudden death

==== SLE (Recurrent Abortion + Malar Rash) – ক্লিনিক্যাল সিনারিও
- Diagnosis: SLE ± Antiphospholipid Syndrome
- 4 Investigations:
  1. ANA – sensitive (>95%)
  2. Anti-dsDNA – specific, correlates with nephritis
  3. Anti-Smith (Sm) – highly specific
  4. Complement C3/C4 – low in active disease
- Additional: Anti-phospholipid antibodies (Lupus anticoagulant, Anti-cardiolipin, Anti-β2GP1)
- Drug: Hydroxychloroquine (first-line) ± Steroids (severe)

==== Bone Pain in SLE 
- Avascular Necrosis (AVN) – most common (steroid-induced)
- Arthralgia/Arthritis – non-erosive, small joints
- Myalgia
- Osteoporosis (chronic steroids)

==== Monoarthritis Treated with Steroids
- Definition: Single joint inflammation
- Steroid-responsive causes:
  - Gout (if NSAIDs/Colchicine contraindicated)
  - Psoriatic arthritis
  - Reactive arthritis
  - SLE arthritis
  - Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (oligoarticular)
- RED FLAG (NOT steroids alone): Septic arthritis → IV antibiotics + drainage

==== Causes of Sudden Severe Headache 
- Subarachnoid Hemorrhage (SAH) – thunderclap, worst headache
- Meningitis/Encephalitis – fever, neck stiffness, photophobia
- Migraine – pulsatile, unilateral, ± aura
- Hypertensive emergency – BP >180/120, papilledema
- Cluster headache – orbital pain, autonomic symptoms
- Acute Glaucoma – red eye, painful, halos
- Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis – progressive headache, focal deficits

==== Causes of Severe Breathlessness 
- Pulmonary Embolism (PE) – sudden, pleuritic, hemoptysis
- Acute Pulmonary Edema (HF) – orthopnea, PND, crackles, S3
- COPD/Asthma exacerbation – wheeze, prolonged expiration
- Pneumothorax – sudden, hyperresonant, decreased breath sounds
- Pneumonia – fever, cough, crackles, consolidation
- Anaphylaxis – stridor, urticaria, hypotension
- Foreign body aspiration – choking, localized wheeze

==== Fever with Chills & Rigor 
- Sepsis – qSOFA ≥2
- Malaria – tertian/quartan fever, travel history, splenomegaly
- UTI (Pyelonephritis) – dysuria, flank pain, CVA tenderness
- Pneumonia – cough, sputum, infiltrate
- Dengue – high fever, retro-orbital pain, myalgia, rash, thrombocytopenia
- Typhoid – step-ladder fever, relative bradycardia, rose spots
- Infective Endocarditis – fever, new murmur, embolic phenomena

==== Oligoarthritis 
- Definition: 2-4 joints involved
- Features: Asymmetric, lower limb predominant
- Causes:
  - Reactive Arthritis – post-enteric/urogenital, HLA-B27+, ± conjunctivitis/urethritis
  - Psoriatic Arthritis – asymmetric, ± dactylitis, nail pitting
  - SLE – non-erosive
  - Gout – podagra, tophi
  - TB Arthritis – chronic, monoarticular
  - JIA – oligoarticular type
- Treatment: NSAIDs ± Steroids (after ruling out septic arthritis)

==== MI to Heart Failure (Pathophysiology – বাংলায় বুঝতে হবে)
- Myocardial dysfunction – necrosis >20% LV → reduced EF
- Infarct size >40% → cardiogenic shock
- Neurohormonal activation – RAAS, SNS → vasoconstriction, fluid retention
- Ventricular remodeling – wall thinning, dilation, fibrosis
- Papillary muscle dysfunction → acute MR
- Ventricular septal rupture → acute VSD
- Pericarditis → tamponade (rare)
- Clinical: Pulmonary edema, S3, elevated JVP, hypotension, oliguria

=== Board 2 - Respiratory & Hematology

==== Pleural Effusion: X-ray Findings
- Costophrenic angle blunting (earliest)
- Homogeneous opacity with concave upper border (meniscus sign)
- Massive effusion → complete opacification ± mediastinal shift
- Lateral decubitus → free-flowing vs loculated

==== 3 Causes of Pleural Effusion
1. Tuberculosis – exudative, lymphocyte-rich, high ADA
2. Malignancy – exudative, hemorrhagic, low pH/glucose
3. Pneumonia (Parapneumonic) – exudative, neutrophil-rich, ± empyema

==== Unilateral vs Bilateral Pleural Effusion
- Unilateral: Malignancy, TB, Parapneumonic, PE, Pancreatitis, Rheumatoid, Mesothelioma
- Bilateral: Heart failure (most common), Hypoalbuminemia, SLE, Uremia

==== Investigations for Pleural Effusion
1. CXR (PA & Lateral)
2. Ultrasound (guide thoracentesis)
3. Pleural fluid analysis (biochemistry, cytology, microbiology)
4. CT thorax
5. Pleural biopsy

==== Exudative vs Transudative (Light's Criteria)
- Exudate if ANY ONE:
  1. Pleural fluid protein / Serum protein > 0.5
  2. Pleural fluid LDH / Serum LDH > 0.6
  3. Pleural fluid LDH > 2/3 upper normal serum LDH
- Exudate: High protein (>3g/dL), high LDH (>200), low glucose (< 60), low pH (< 7.3), high WBC
- Transudate: Low protein (< 3g/dL), low LDH (< 200), glucose same as serum, pH 7.4-7.5

==== Biochemical & Cytological Parameters
- Protein: Exudate >3g/dL; Transudate < 3g/dL
- LDH: Exudate >200 IU/L
- Glucose: < 60mg/dL in TB, malignancy, rheumatoid, empyema
- pH: < 7.3 in empyema, malignancy, rheumatoid (drain if < 7.2)
- Cell count:
  - Neutrophils >50% → Parapneumonic, PE
  - Lymphocytes >50% → TB, Malignancy
  - Eosinophils → PE, drug-induced
  - RBC → Hemorrhagic (malignancy, trauma)
- Microbiology: Gram stain, culture, AFB
- Cytology: Malignant cells

==== TB Drugs (RIPE) – ডোজ ও কমপ্লিকেশন
| Drug | Dose | Complication  |
|------|------|------------------------|
| Isoniazid (H) | 5mg/kg (max 300mg) | Peripheral neuropathy → give Pyridoxine (Vit B6) |
| Rifampicin (R) | 10mg/kg (max 600mg) | Hepatotoxicity, orange-red body fluids, CYP450 inducer |
| Pyrazinamide (Z) | 25mg/kg (max 2g) | Hyperuricemia → gout, hepatotoxicity |
| Ethambutol (E) | 15mg/kg (max 1.2g) | Optic neuritis (red-green color blindness) – contraindicated < 5 years |
- Regimen: 2HRZE + 4HR
- Monitor: LFT, visual acuity, uric acid

==== Multiple Myeloma – সম্পূর্ণ
- X-ray: Punched-out lytic lesions (skull, spine, pelvis, ribs)
- Investigations:
  1. SPEP – M-protein spike
  2. Urine Bence Jones – free light chains
  3. Bone marrow – >10% plasma cells
  4. Serum free light chain assay
  5. Cytogenetics/FISH – risk stratification
- Cell type: Plasma cells
- CRAB Criteria:
  - C – Calcium high (>11.5mg/dL)
  - R – Renal failure (Cr >2mg/dL)
  - A – Anemia (Hb < 10g/dL)
  - B – Bone lesions
- Treatment:
  - Chemotherapy (CyBorD: Cyclophosphamide + Bortezomib + Dexamethasone)
  - Proteasome inhibitors (Bortezomib, Carfilzomib)
  - Immunomodulators (Lenalidomide, Pomalidomide)
  - Autologous stem cell transplant
  - Supportive: Bisphosphonates, Erythropoietin, antibiotics
- Differential Diagnosis:
  - MGUS – no CRAB, M-protein < 3g/dL
  - Waldenström Macroglobulinemia – IgM, hyperviscosity
  - Amyloidosis – Congo red positive, apple-green birefringence
  - Metastatic carcinoma – osteolytic (breast, lung) ± osteoblastic (prostate)
  - Lymphoma – nodal, B-symptoms

==== Hypercalcemia Causes 
- PTHrP-mediated (malignancy – squamous cell, breast, renal)
- Osteolytic lesions (myeloma, metastatic carcinoma)
- Vitamin D excess (sarcoidosis, TB, lymphoma)
- Primary Hyperparathyroidism – most common outpatient cause
- Granulomatous diseases (TB, sarcoidosis)
- Thiazide diuretics
- Milk-alkali syndrome
- Immobilization
- Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH)

==== Hyponatremia (Na < 120 with Drowsiness)
- Diagnosis: Severe symptomatic hyponatremia
- Treatment: Hypertonic saline (3% NaCl)
- Protocol:
  - Goal: Increase Na by 4-6 mEq/L in first 4-6 hours
  - Rate: 1-2 mL/kg/hour (bolus 100mL over 10-15 min if seizures)
  - Maximum correction: < 8-10 mEq/L in 24 hours (avoid ODS)
  - Monitor: Serum Na every 2-4 hours
- Causes: SIADH, adrenal insufficiency, hypothyroidism, HF, cirrhosis, diuretics

=== Board 3 - Dermatology & Paediatrics

==== Acne Vulgaris – সম্পূর্ণ
- Pathogenesis:
  1. Androgen → increased sebum
  2. Sebum → oily skin
  3. Hyperkeratinization → follicular plugging
  4. C. acnes → lipase → free fatty acids → inflammation
  5. Inflammation → papules, pustules, nodules
- Stages:
  1. Comedonal (blackheads/whiteheads)
  2. Papulopustular (moderate)
  3. Nodular (severe, painful)
  4. Cystic (severest, scarring)
- Treatment:
  - Mild: Topical retinoids (Tretinoin, Adapalene)
  - Moderate: Topical retinoid + Benzoyl peroxide + Topical antibiotics (Clindamycin)
  - Severe: Oral Isotretinoin (0.5-1mg/kg/day, 4-6 months) – teratogenic
  - Systemic antibiotics: Doxycycline, Minocycline
  - Hormonal: COCP (women)

==== Psychiatry Basics – সংজ্ঞা
- Delirium:
  - Acute onset, fluctuating course, attention disturbance
  - Types: Hyperactive, Hypoactive, Mixed
- Hallucinations: Perception without stimulus (visual, auditory, tactile)
- Illusions: Misinterpretation of real stimulus
- Delirium Management:
  - Treat underlying cause
  - Non-pharmacological: Reorientation, calm environment
  - Pharmacological: Low-dose Haloperidol (0.5-2mg) – avoid benzodiazepines

==== Urticaria (Hives) – Management
- Avoid triggers (NSAIDs, foods, physical stimuli, stress)
- Antihistamines: Second-generation (Cetirizine, Loratadine) – first-line
- Severe/Chronic: Steroids (Prednisolone) short course, Montelukast, Omalizumab
- Acute anaphylaxis: Epinephrine IM, IV fluids, antihistamines, steroids, oxygen

==== Vitamin D, C, A Deficiencies – X-ray Findings
- Vitamin D (Rickets):
  - Cupping, fraying, widened growth plate
  - Bowing (tibia/femur), osteopenia
  - Clinical: Craniotabes, Harrison's sulcus, rachitic rosary
- Vitamin C (Scurvy):
  - Subperiosteal hemorrhage, scorbutic rosary
  - Frankel's line (dense white at metaphysis)
  - Trümmerfeld zone (radiolucent band)
  - Wimberger's sign (ring-like epiphysis) – pathognomonic
  - Clinical: Bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, petechiae
- Vitamin A (Hypervitaminosis):
  - Cortical hyperostosis (periosteal new bone formation)
  - Clinical: Hepatomegaly, pseudotumor cerebri, dry skin, alopecia

==== Thalassemia – X-ray & Investigation
- X-ray: "Hair-on-end" appearance in skull (marrow expansion)
- Other: Medullary expansion, bossing, chipmunk facies
- Blood: Microcytic hypochromic anemia, target cells, basophilic stippling
- Investigation: Hb Electrophoresis – HbA2 >3.5% (beta-thal trait)

==== Nephrotic Syndrome – সম্পূর্ণ
- 4 features:
  1. Heavy proteinuria (>3.5g/day) – frothy urine
  2. Hypoalbuminemia (< 3g/dL)
  3. Edema (periorbital, pedal, ascites, anasarca)
  4. Hyperlipidemia (high cholesterol, LDL, VLDL)
- Investigations:
  - Urinalysis: Proteinuria 3+/4+, ± hematuria, fatty casts, oval fat bodies
  - Serum albumin: Low
  - Serum lipids: High
  - Renal biopsy: Definitive diagnosis
  - Other: BUN/Cr, complement C3/C4, ANA, HBV/HCV, HIV
- Complications: Infection, thromboembolism, AKI, malnutrition

==== Acute Glomerulonephritis (AGN) vs Nephrotic Syndrome
| Feature | AGN | Nephrotic |
|---------|-----|-----------|
| Proteinuria | Mild-moderate (< 3g/day) | Heavy (>3.5g/day) |
| Hematuria | Gross/microscopic (RBC casts) | Microscopic (occasional) |
| Albumin | Normal/low-normal | Low (< 3g/dL) |
| Lipids | Normal | High |
| Edema | Mild | Severe (anasarca) |
| Hypertension | Common | Less common |
| Oliguria | Common | Less common |
| Treatment | Supportive ± dialysis | Steroids, immunosuppressants |

==== Acute Viral Nephritis – Child Death Causes
1. Hyperkalemia – arrhythmia
2. Pulmonary edema – fluid overload
3. Uremic encephalopathy – confusion, seizures, coma
4. Severe hypertension – hypertensive encephalopathy
5. Severe acidosis
6. Superimposed sepsis
- Management: Dialysis, IV insulin + calcium gluconate + NaHCO3 for hyperkalemia, fluid restriction, antihypertensives

==== Neonatal Mortality in Bangladesh – ৪টি প্রধান কারণ
1. Prematurity (low birth weight, RDS, IVH)
2. Birth asphyxia (HIE, meconium aspiration)
3. Neonatal sepsis (GBS, E. coli, Listeria)
4. Congenital anomalies (CHD, NTD, diaphragmatic hernia)
- Others: Hypothermia, hypoglycemia, jaundice, pneumonia, meningitis

==== Neonatal Jaundice – সম্পূর্ণ
- Causes :
  - Physiological (day 2-3, resolves day 10-14)
  - Breast milk jaundice (day 5-7, peaks week 2)
  - Hemolytic: ABO/Rh incompatibility (Coombs+), G6PD deficiency
  - Sepsis – conjugated/unconjugated
  - Biliary atresia – conjugated, pale stools, dark urine (surgical emergency)
  - Prematurity – immature liver
  - Crigler-Najjar syndrome (type I severe, type II mild)
  - Gilbert syndrome – benign
- Investigations:
  - Serum bilirubin (total, direct, indirect)
  - CBC (anemia, hemolysis)
  - Coombs test (direct)
  - G6PD assay
  - Reticulocyte count
  - Blood group & Rh (mother + baby)
  - LFT (AST/ALT, albumin)
  - Ultrasound (biliary atresia)
  - Infection workup (CRP, blood culture, urine culture)
- Treatment:
  - Phototherapy (blue light 450-470nm)
  - Exchange transfusion (severe >25mg/dL term, >20mg/dL preterm)
  - IVIG (hemolytic disease)
  - Treat underlying cause (antibiotics, surgery)
  - Supportive: Hydration, nutrition, avoid displacing drugs

==== Bone Marrow Aspiration – সম্পূর্ণ
- Needle: Jamshidi (biopsy), Salah (aspiration), Spinal (pediatric)
- Sites: Posterior iliac crest (most common), sternum, anterior iliac crest, tibia (neonates)
- Indications:
  - Diagnosis: Leukemia, lymphoma, multiple myeloma, aplastic anemia, MDS
  - Staging: Lymphoma, neuroblastoma
  - FUO: Leishmaniasis, TB, fungal
  - Storage disorders: Gaucher's, Niemann-Pick
  - Follow-up: Post-chemo response, transplant
- Contraindications: Bleeding diathesis (platelets < 20k), infection at site, severe osteoporosis

==== Spacer Use – বিস্তারিত
- Device with MDI – improves lung deposition (10-15% → 30-40%)
- Reduces oropharyngeal deposition → fewer side effects (candidiasis, hoarseness)
- Helpful for children/elderly (poor coordination)
- How to use: Shake MDI, insert in spacer, actuate once, inhale slowly within 5 seconds, hold breath 5-10 seconds, wait 30 seconds before next puff

==== Inhaler – Blue (Salbutamol)
- Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol) – SABA (Short-Acting Beta-2 Agonist)
- Action: β2 agonist → bronchodilation (↑cAMP)
- Uses: Acute bronchospasm in asthma/COPD (rescue)
- Onset: 3-5 minutes
- Dose: 2-4 puffs (100µg/puff) – repeat after 20 minutes
- Side effects: Tachycardia, tremor, hypokalemia

==== Ambu Bag – সম্পূর্ণ
- Full meaning: Ambu (company name – "Ambulance" bag)
- Function: Bag Valve Mask (BVM) – manual positive pressure ventilation
- Uses: Respiratory failure, cardiac arrest, pre-oxygenation, transport ventilation
- Components: Self-inflating bag, one-way valve, reservoir (oxygen port)

==== NG Tube – কীভাবে দিতে হয়
- Measurement: Nose → Ear → Xiphisternum
- Confirmation: Auscultation over epigastrium (air insufflation) OR pH testing (gastric pH < 5.5)
- Uses: Feeding, decompression, drug administration, lavage

==== Lumbar Puncture – কীভাবে দিতে হয়
- Position: Lateral decubitus (fetal position)
- Site: L3-L4 or L4-L5 (below conus medullaris)
- Opening pressure: Manometer (normal 5-20 cmH2O)
- CSF in GBS: Albumino-cytological dissociation (high protein, normal WBC)
- Contraindications (LP করবেন না):
  - Raised ICP (herniation risk) → CT brain first
  - Infection at site
  - Bleeding diathesis (platelets < 50k)
- Causes of herniation: Space-occupying lesion, obstructive hydrocephalus, cerebral edema

==== Chromosomal Diseases – ৩টি
1. Down Syndrome (Trisomy 21)
   - Investigation: Karyotyping
   - Findings: Upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthic folds, single palmar crease, hypotonia, macroglossia, CHD (AVSD), duodenal atresia
   - Associated: Hypothyroidism, Celiac disease, ALCL (leukemia)
2. Turner Syndrome (45, XO)
   - Features: Short stature, webbed neck, shield chest, ovarian dysgenesis, coarctation of aorta
3. Klinefelter Syndrome (47, XXY)
   - Features: Tall stature, gynecomastia, small testes, azoospermia, cognitive impairment

==== Scurvy Pictogram Findings
- Subperiosteal hemorrhage (calcified periosteal elevation)
- Scorbutic rosary (sharp costochondral junctions)
- Frankel's line (dense white metaphyseal line)
- Trümmerfeld zone (radiolucent band)
- Wimberger's sign (ring-like epiphysis) – pathognomonic
- Clinical: Bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, petechiae, coiled hair

==== Rickets Pictogram Findings
- Cupping, fraying, widened growth plate
- Bowing (genu varum/valgum)
- Osteopenia, cortical thinning
- Clinical: Craniotabes, Harrison's sulcus, rachitic rosary, delayed fontanelle

==== Thalassemia Pictogram
- Hair-on-end skull (diploic widening, trabecular striations)
- Medullary expansion, cortex thinning, bossing, chipmunk facies

==== Pruritus Causes 
- Cholestasis (bile salts deposition)
- Uremia (calcium/phosphate deposition)
- Iron deficiency
- Skin disease (eczema, psoriasis, scabies)
- Polycythemia vera (aquagenic pruritus)
- Thyroid disease (hyperthyroidism)
- Malignancy (lymphoma, leukemia)
- Drugs (opioids, statins)

==== Graves' Disease – Mechanism
- Autoantibody: TSI (Thyroid Stimulating Immunoglobulin)
- Binds to TSH receptor → mimics TSH → unregulated thyroid hormone production
- Diffuse goiter, ophthalmopathy (exophthalmos), pretibial myxedema

==== Thyrotoxicosis Causes
- Graves' disease (most common)
- Toxic multinodular goiter
- Toxic adenoma
- Subacute thyroiditis (de Quervain) – painful, transient
- Hashitoxicosis (Hashimoto's with transient thyrotoxicosis)
- Amiodarone-induced (type 1 or 2)
- Factitious thyrotoxicosis (exogenous thyroid hormone)
- Struma ovarii (teratoma with thyroid tissue)

==== Jaundice – Types 
- Pre-hepatic (Unconjugated): Hemolysis (spherocytosis, G6PD, malaria)
- Hepatic (Mixed/Conjugated): Viral hepatitis, Alcoholic liver disease, Drugs, Wilson's disease
- Post-hepatic (Conjugated): Obstructive – Stone, Tumor, Stricture, Pancreatic cancer

==== UTI – Clinical Features 
- Dysuria, Frequency, Urgency, Suprapubic pain, Hematuria
- Pyelonephritis: Fever, chills, flank pain, CVA tenderness, nausea/vomiting
- Complicated: Pregnancy (honeymoon cystitis), Catheter-associated, Structural/anatomical
- Investigation: Urine R/E, Urine culture sensitivity

==== Prescription – সম্পূর্ণ ফরম্যাট
- Patient: Name, Age, Sex, Weight
- Date
- Superscription: Rx
- Inscription: Drug name, dose, strength, route, frequency
- Subscription: Dispensing instructions (quantity, form)
- Signature (Sig): How to take (e.g., "1 tablet twice daily after meals")
- Refill information (if needed)
- Prescriber's signature & registration number
- Note: Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) – part of LFT/bone profile – monitor in liver/bone disease

==== Peripheral Pulse Examination (8 points – সিরিয়াল ওয়াইজ)
1. Radial
2. Brachial
3. Femoral
4. Popliteal
5. Posterior tibial
6. Dorsalis pedis
7. (Optional) Carotid
8. (Optional) Temporal
- Assess: Presence/Absence, Rate, Rhythm, Volume, Character (e.g., bounding in AR, thready in shock)
- Symmetry: Compare both sides
- Radio-femoral delay: Coarctation of aorta
- Peripheral vascular disease: Absent/reduced pulses, bruits

==== Examine Hand – Findings (শর্ট কেস)
- Clubbing (stages 1-4)
- Cyanosis (central